# Line endings, decided here rather than by each machine's Git settings.
#
# This file exists because of one specific failure. Git for Windows converts
# text files to CRLF on checkout by default. A shell script converted that way
# still looks fine in an editor, but its first line becomes
#
#     #!/usr/bin/env bash\r
#
# and Linux looks for an interpreter literally called "bash\r". Inside a
# container that is an immediate exit with "no such file or directory" — and
# from the outside all anybody sees is `container archeo-api is unhealthy`,
# which names neither the file nor the reason.
#
# The scripts in this repository are copied into Linux containers, so they must
# be LF wherever they were checked out. The Windows launchers must be CRLF for
# the same reason in reverse: cmd.exe mis-parses an LF-only batch file.
#
# After changing this file, run `git add --renormalize .` so what is already
# committed is brought into line rather than only new work.

# Default: Git decides what is text, and text is stored and checked out as LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Runs on Linux, whatever machine it was checked out on.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile text eol=lf
docker/** text eol=lf
backend/docker/** text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Runs on Windows. cmd.exe needs CRLF; PowerShell tolerates either but is kept
# consistent with the launchers beside it.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary: never converted, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.xlsx binary
*.shp binary
*.dbf binary
*.shx binary
